course: dictionary
lesson: 4531
title: Healthcare
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Healthcare is the work of looking after people's health. It includes doctors, nurses, hospitals, medicine, and the help people get when they are ill or hurt. It is a noun that does not change into a plural, so we say healthcare, not healthcares.
explain: Use the context to find its meaning. «In the UK the NHS, set up in 1948, gives most healthcare free at the point of use, paid for by taxes.» Free at the point of use means you do not pay when you are treated, and the taxes show that everyone pays a little towards it. Swap in your meaning: "gives most help for illness free" still makes sense.
explain: Healthcare often appears in phrases such as healthcare worker, free healthcare and access to healthcare. Access means being able to get something. «People disagree about how much healthcare should be public or private» shows that people argue about how healthcare is run, so read the whole sentence to be sure of its meaning.
fact: Healthcare means the work of looking after people's health.
fact: Healthcare is uncountable: we say some healthcare or much healthcare, not a healthcare or healthcares.
fact: «Healthcare» includes doctors, nurses, hospitals and medicine.
fact: The NHS, set up in 1948, is a healthcare service in the UK that gives most healthcare free at the point of use.
fact: Some countries provide healthcare publicly, some privately, and many mix both.
exercise: «In the UK the NHS gives most healthcare free at the point of use.» What does healthcare mean here? | The paint and tools used to build hospitals | A type of free food | The taxes people pay each year | *The work of looking after people's health
exercise: Which of these is a healthcare worker? | A baker making bread | *A nurse looking after patients in a hospital | A postman delivering letters | A teacher in a primary school
exercise: In «access to healthcare», what does access mean? | A kind of medicine | A locked door | Being unable to see | *Being able to get something
test: In the NHS sentence, in which year was the NHS set up? | 1984 | 1848 | 1958 | *1948
test: What part of speech is healthcare? | *Noun | Adverb | Adjective | Verb
exam: Which sentence uses healthcare correctly? | Healthcare is a fruit that grows on trees | Many countries mix public and private healthcares | Healthcare is a loud word for a noisy hospital | *Many countries mix public and private healthcare
